  {
    "episode_id": 72743,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72743.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 3,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think we're pretty unified. People were very aggressive in their questions. They were not aimed in particular at Greg or Ted, but we had the economist there trying to explain these mitigation payments and the criteria behind them. They're going to issue a white paper. I told them to get it up here just as fast as we possibly can. I think there's a lot of head scratch going on in farm country, understanding that if we could have a breakthrough with China and all of the things we would like to see happen with China to have them actually fulfill their WTO obligations. And there's just a whole bunch of things that they are doing where they could improve dramatically. I mean, we all know that. And so if we could get that done, that would certainly be a positive in the meantime. What has happened to us, of course, is that if you have some kind of a trade dispute, then that country comes back and retaliates, and guess who's a bullseye? That's agriculture. And we've seen it across the board in agriculture with a lot of trade retaliation. That's why they had their mitigation payments. We need to really work on a consistent and predictable trade policy. We don't need to be losing markets. They're very difficult to get back. And we do do everything we can. One thing I think we ought to really take a look at is rejoining TPP. I know the president is indicating he wants to do bilateral bilateral bilateral. But there's 11 of them over there. If you want to send a message to China, especially when they're having their blue water Navy go out 200 miles from uh... the mainland and they're doing uh... military exercises with the Russians for kind of sake i think we got a senate message would be to do business with the tpp countries they don't want to do business with china they want to do business with us and then that also means we would have their back I think that'd be a strong message portrayed and I think that'd be a very strong message for national security.",
