    "turn_idx": 15,
    "turn_text": "Yes, there is that opportunity, but Jeff, we have work requirements in place now and we have work requirements in the Senate bill. The Senate bill improves the integrity of the SNAP program, and that also protects access, or access, pardon me, for those who really need the help. And we do that by increasing the oversight of the states. We have better accuracy in tracking, better reporting of SNAP error rates, ending the $42 million bonus payments where the states were gaming the system. We have a great deal of reform, and we also modernize the SNAP verification process. All that gets lost. There it took me, what, 30 seconds to go into all that, and there's more. We have very strict work requirements. Also, we have to address the issue of waivers, and I know the UNB is going to come with their version of what they think an executive order by Secretary Purdue would look like. We could, I think, work with those folks before we decide on our own what that might be. But the Senate bill increases accountability to improve SNAP, and we do have work requirements. What we don't do is increase the ages on both sides of people that would be or that have kids it would be ten and under as opposed to six and under and then on the other side fifty to fifty nine uh... we can't we can't pass a bill uh... with those kind of uh... requirements that were in the house bill uh... in the senate and yet sixty votes",
