  {
    "episode_id": 72743,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72743.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 23,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think one is a suggestion that we change the acreage base requirements or eligibility. And I think that's a discussion that needs to, we really need to further discuss that. That money would be used to go back and fix a problem, clear back 2008 to 2012. But that's just one. There are others with regards to reference prices. That's pretty expensive. I think probably the biggest one is they snap provisions And in that regard, we see a lot of press by people who are observing this and making observations with the idea that the Senate bill has no work requirements. We have work requirements now. And we think our bill makes the work requirements work better. And I think if we can make our point on that, I might point out that it was that bill that got a vote of 86 to 11. uh... the other bill in the house uh... was passed by two votes and it was a complete uh... partisan bill i'm not going to knock that out if that was the only way that uh... that they could get that done by that's the only way that they could get that done the only way you get it done in the senate to get sixty votes is to be bipartisan and so uh... i think people have to understand that and i i hope they're finally coming to that uh... realization",
